/*!
    \page getting-started.html
    \title Getting Started
    \previouspage Introduction and Overview
    \nextpage OPC UA Client

    This chapter walks through starting the application and reaching the point where
    you can browse a server or design one.

    \section1 Starting the application

    Launch the installed application (or the portable build's executable). On start the
    program opens the \e launcher, from which you can:

    \list
        \li create a new client project or open a recent one;
        \li open an existing client project (\c .uaproj) from disk;
        \li open \e{Server Studio} to design or run a local OPC UA server.
    \endlist

    A \e project is optional for a quick look: you can connect to a server without
    saving a project. A project becomes useful when you want to keep a connection and a
    set of monitored nodes for next time. See \l {Configuration} for what a project
    stores and where.

    \section1 Your first client connection

    \list 1
        \li From the launcher, create or open a client project (or continue without one).
        \li In the connection form, enter the server's discovery URL, for example
            \c {opc.tcp://127.0.0.1:4840}.
        \li Choose the security policy and the identity (anonymous, or username/password)
            the server expects, then connect.
        \li When the connection succeeds, the server's address space appears in the
            browse tree. Continue in \l {OPC UA Client}.
    \endlist

    \section1 Your first local server

    \list 1
        \li From the launcher, open \e{Server Studio}.
        \li Create a server project (\c .uaserver) and add a namespace, objects, and
            variables to the address space.
        \li Start the server; the studio launches the headless runtime as a separate
            process and reports its endpoint.
        \li Use \e{Open in Client} to connect the built-in client to your running server.
            Continue in \l {OPC UA Server} and \l {Working with a Server}.
    \endlist

    \section1 Choosing the UI language

    The application ships with English, German, French, Italian, Russian, and Ukrainian
    user interfaces.
    The language can be switched at runtime and is remembered between sessions
    (see \l {Configuration}). This documentation is available in the same languages;
    the API reference is English.

    \section1 Opening this documentation

    This guide ships with the application and opens from \e{Info > Documentation}. It is
    shown in an offline documentation window that needs no internet connection and follows
    the application theme; navigation controls let you go back, forward, reload, and return
    to the guide's home page. The same window also hosts the \l {OpcUaManager API Reference}.
*/
